@@ -837,10 +837,44 @@ Manage records without starting a model, from the project directory:
 
 ```sh
 kiln memory export                 # JSON, including correction history
+kiln memory import backup.json     # import an export into this project (see below)
 kiln memory forget test-runner     # deletes every version of this key
 kiln memory restore 12             # restores this project's version as a new record
 ```
 
+`import` reads exactly one `kiln memory export` JSON array and loads it into the
+current project scope (or the global preferences scope with `kiln memory global
+import`). It is a local command and never starts a model. The input is validated
+in full before anything is written, and the whole selected batch is applied in
+one transaction, so a rejected import leaves memory, search and source bindings
+untouched.
+
+```sh
+kiln memory import backup.json                          # error if any key already exists (default)
+kiln memory import backup.json --on-conflict skip       # keep existing keys, import the rest
+kiln memory import backup.json --on-conflict replace    # supersede existing keys, keeping their history
+kiln memory import backup.json --dry-run                # validate and report counts without writing
+kiln memory global import prefs.json                    # into the global preferences scope
+```
+
+An export carries a key's correction history; import validates every version,
+then takes the record with the largest id for each key as its current value.
+Imported scope, ids, creation times and supersedes links are never trusted:
+records land in the destination scope with fresh ids and creation times, their
+expiry preserved, and are superseded against the destination's own history. A
+conflict is any existing active key in the destination (even expired, even with
+a stale source). `error` rejects the whole batch, `skip` leaves conflicting keys
+alone, and `replace` creates a new current record while preserving local
+correction history. Project source bindings (at most eight files per record) are
+re-rooted into the current project, and each file's SHA-256 is re-verified
+against the current worktree; a missing, changed, redirected, oversized or
+escaping source rejects the import. Global import accepts preference records
+without file sources only. On success `import` prints a single JSON object with
+integer `inserted`, `replaced` and `skipped` counts and a boolean `dry_run`
+(dry-run counts describe what would happen); on failure it exits nonzero with a
+diagnostic and writes nothing. Inputs over 4 MiB or carrying more than 1000
+records are refused; an empty array is valid.
+
 Explicit user preferences can be shared across projects:
 
 ```sh
